const PRACTICAL_NOTES={"10-02":["Why choose acetaminophen?","Useful for mild-to-moderate pain or fever when you want a non-opioid option without the platelet inhibition and GI/renal concerns of NSAIDs. It is not as strong as an opioid for severe traumatic pain, but it can reduce opioid need in appropriate patients.","Best fit: stable patient with mild/moderate pain or fever when an opioid is unnecessary.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34928760/"],"10-03":["Why choose aspirin?","For suspected acute coronary syndrome, aspirin is different from pain medicines: its key value is antiplatelet activity. It helps limit further platelet-driven clot formation, so it is chosen for suspected ACS rather than simply for analgesia.","Best fit: suspected ACS when the county protocol indicates antiplatelet therapy.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519032/"],"10-04":["Why choose adenosine?","Adenosine acts extremely fast at the AV node and has a very short half-life. That makes it useful when a regular narrow-complex tachycardia is likely AV-node dependent: you can get rapid conversion or diagnostic information without a long-lasting drug effect.","Best fit: a stable, regular SVT-type rhythm when the protocol criteria are met.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519049/"],"10-05":["Why choose albuterol?","Albuterol directly relaxes bronchial smooth muscle and is inhaled, so it targets bronchospasm with less systemic adrenergic effect than epinephrine. Epinephrine becomes more attractive when bronchospasm is part of anaphylaxis or is extremely severe/refractory under protocol.","Best fit: wheezing/bronchospasm where a beta-2 bronchodilator is the main need.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482272/"],"10-06":["Why choose amiodarone?","Amiodarone has activity across several cardiac ion channels and is used for ventricular dysrhythmias. Compared with procainamide, the choice depends heavily on rhythm, stability, structural heart disease, QT/QRS considerations, and the local algorithm rather than one drug being universally better.","Best fit: ventricular dysrhythmia when the specific protocol calls for amiodarone.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482154/"],"10-07":["Why choose atropine?","Atropine blocks parasympathetic slowing of the heart. It is useful when symptomatic bradycardia is likely to respond to vagal blockade; pacing or adrenergic support may be more appropriate when atropine is ineffective or the conduction problem is unlikely to respond.","Best fit: symptomatic bradycardia that meets the protocol criteria.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470551/"],"10-08":["Why choose buprenorphine?","Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with very high receptor affinity. In EMS its advantage is not routine acute analgesia versus fentanyl or morphine; it is particularly useful when the goal is treating opioid withdrawal and beginning medication treatment for opioid use disorder.","Best fit: opioid withdrawal/OUD treatment when the county protocol criteria are met.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459126/"],"10-09":["Why choose calcium chloride?","Calcium chloride delivers more elemental calcium per typical volume than calcium gluconate, so it is useful when rapid, concentrated calcium delivery is important. The tradeoff is that it is more irritating if it extravasates, making calcium gluconate attractive when tissue injury risk is a bigger concern.","Best fit: time-critical hyperkalemia/toxicologic or resuscitation situations when protocol calls for concentrated calcium.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32022081/"],"10-10":["Why choose calcium gluconate?","Calcium gluconate provides calcium with less tissue injury risk if extravasation occurs than calcium chloride. The tradeoff is that it contains less elemental calcium per typical volume, so more volume may be needed for an equivalent calcium load.","Best fit: calcium is indicated but peripheral-line tissue safety is an important consideration.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32022081/"],"10-11":["Why choose cefazolin?","Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin with strong activity against many common skin flora. In trauma systems it is commonly selected for prophylaxis in open fractures because it gives useful gram-positive coverage without using an unnecessarily broad antibiotic.","Best fit: injury patterns where the county protocol specifically calls for early antibiotic prophylaxis.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542228/"],"10-12":["Why choose dexamethasone?","Dexamethasone is a potent, long-acting corticosteroid. Compared with shorter-acting steroids, its long duration is useful when you want sustained anti-inflammatory effect, such as croup; it does not replace epinephrine for immediate life-threatening anaphylaxis.","Best fit: inflammatory airway conditions where a long steroid effect is useful.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482130/"],"10-13":["Why choose dextrose?","Dextrose directly supplies glucose and can rapidly correct symptomatic hypoglycemia. Compared with glucagon, IV/IO dextrose generally works more directly when vascular access is available; glucagon is useful when access is not available but depends on glycogen stores.","Best fit: symptomatic hypoglycemia with IV/IO access and protocol indication.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545201/"],"10-14":["Why choose diazepam?","Diazepam is a benzodiazepine with rapid CNS penetration and a longer clinical tail than midazolam. In EMS, midazolam is often operationally easier because of reliable IM/IN use; diazepam can be useful when its longer duration or a specific protocol indication is desirable.","Best fit: a benzodiazepine indication where the protocol specifically favors diazepam.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537022/"],"10-15":["Why choose diltiazem?","Diltiazem slows AV-nodal conduction and can control ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation/flutter. It is chosen for rate control rather than conversion of an unstable patient; hypotension or certain heart-failure situations may make another strategy preferable.","Best fit: stable AF/flutter with rapid ventricular response when protocol criteria are met.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532937/"],"10-16":["Why choose diphenhydramine?","Diphenhydramine blocks H1 histamine effects and can help itching, hives, and some allergic symptoms. It is an adjunct\u2014not a substitute for epinephrine in anaphylaxis\u2014because it does not rapidly reverse airway edema or shock.","Best fit: histamine-mediated symptoms after the immediate life threats are addressed.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526010/"],"10-17":["Why choose droperidol?","Droperidol is useful for significant agitation because it can provide effective sedation without relying only on a benzodiazepine. Evidence suggests droperidol is an effective option for acute agitation; ketamine may be favored when extremely rapid control is required, while benzodiazepines are especially useful for causes such as withdrawal or stimulant toxicity.","Best fit: significant agitation where antipsychotic sedation matches the suspected cause and protocol.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34823192/"],"10-18":["Why choose epinephrine?","Epinephrine is uniquely useful in anaphylaxis because one drug treats several life threats at once: vasodilation/hypotension, airway swelling, and bronchospasm. Albuterol can help bronchospasm, but it does not treat the shock or airway edema of anaphylaxis.","Best fit: anaphylaxis or another protocol indication requiring alpha and beta adrenergic effects.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482160/"],"10-19":["Why choose etomidate?","Etomidate is an induction sedative known for relatively limited cardiovascular depression compared with many other induction agents. It has no analgesic effect, so ketamine may be more attractive when analgesia or bronchodilation is also desirable.","Best fit: induction when minimizing additional hypotension is a major concern and protocol allows it.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535364/"],"10-20":["Why choose famotidine?","Famotidine blocks H2 histamine receptors. In allergic reactions it is an adjunct for histamine symptoms, not a rescue drug; epinephrine remains the drug that addresses life-threatening anaphylaxis.","Best fit: adjunctive treatment of histamine-mediated symptoms when specified by protocol.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534778/"],"10-21":["Why choose fentanyl?","Fentanyl gives rapid, potent opioid analgesia and causes little histamine release. Compared with morphine, it is often attractive for acute severe pain when you want fast titration and less histamine-related vasodilation; morphine may be attractive when a somewhat longer analgesic effect is useful. Evidence-based EMS guidance considers either fentanyl or morphine reasonable depending on the patient and situation.","Best fit: acute severe pain\u2014especially trauma\u2014when rapid titratable analgesia is desirable and the protocol permits it.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34928760/"],"10-22":["Why choose hydroxocobalamin?","Hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide to form a compound that can be eliminated. Unlike nitrites, it does not intentionally create methemoglobinemia, which is an advantage in smoke-inhalation patients who may also have carbon monoxide exposure and limited oxygen delivery.","Best fit: suspected significant cyanide toxicity, especially compatible smoke-inhalation scenarios.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557486/"],"10-23":["Why choose hydroxyzine?","Hydroxyzine is an antihistamine with anxiolytic and sedating properties. Compared with diphenhydramine, both are sedating H1 blockers; the choice should follow the specific indication and protocol rather than assuming one is universally superior.","Best fit: protocol-defined itching/anxiety/allergic symptom situations where hydroxyzine is specifically listed.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549776/"],"10-24":["Why choose ipratropium?","Ipratropium bronchodilates through an anticholinergic mechanism, so it complements albuterol rather than duplicating it. The combination is useful in significant obstructive bronchospasm because the two drugs open airways through different pathways.","Best fit: moderate/severe asthma or COPD bronchospasm when combined bronchodilation is indicated.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544261/"],"10-25":["Why choose ketamine?","Ketamine can provide analgesia, dissociation, and sedation while often preserving blood pressure better than many sedatives, and it has bronchodilatory properties. For pain it can be an opioid alternative; for extreme agitation it may provide faster control than some alternatives, but it also has important adverse effects and airway monitoring requirements.","Best fit: severe pain when an opioid is undesirable, severe agitation needing rapid control, or induction when its hemodynamic/bronchodilator profile is useful\u2014only where protocol allows.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41760406/"],"10-26":["Why choose ketorolac?","Ketorolac is a non-opioid NSAID, so it can treat inflammatory pain without opioid respiratory depression or sedation. It is especially attractive for pain with an inflammatory component, but renal disease, bleeding risk, GI disease, and other NSAID contraindications matter.","Best fit: appropriate moderate inflammatory/musculoskeletal pain when an opioid is unnecessary or undesirable.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34928760/"],"10-27":["Why choose lidocaine?","Lidocaine can suppress ventricular arrhythmias and can also anesthetize the pain of IO infusion. In ventricular dysrhythmia, whether lidocaine or amiodarone is used depends on the specific algorithm and patient; for IO pain, lidocaine has a separate local-anesthetic role.","Best fit: protocol-defined ventricular dysrhythmia or conscious IO infusion discomfort.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539881/"],"10-28":["Why choose lorazepam?","Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine with a longer anticonvulsant effect than midazolam. Midazolam is often easier for rapid IM/IN prehospital delivery; lorazepam can be attractive when IV/IO access exists and a longer benzodiazepine effect is useful.","Best fit: seizure or sedation indications where longer benzodiazepine activity is desirable and protocol permits it.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532890/"],"10-29":["Why choose magnesium?","Magnesium has several distinct EMS roles: it can stabilize certain ventricular dysrhythmias such as torsades, relax smooth muscle in severe asthma, and is central to eclampsia treatment. It is chosen because of those specific physiologic effects\u2014not as a general antiarrhythmic or bronchodilator.","Best fit: torsades, eclampsia, or severe refractory bronchospasm when the protocol indicates it.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554553/"],"10-30":["Why choose methylprednisolone?","Methylprednisolone is a systemic corticosteroid that reduces inflammation but does not work immediately. It is useful to reduce later inflammatory airway problems; albuterol, epinephrine, and other immediate therapies address the acute life threat first.","Best fit: asthma/COPD or allergic inflammatory conditions where systemic steroid therapy is indicated.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544340/"],"10-31":["Why choose midazolam?","Midazolam has rapid onset and is practical by IV, IM, and intranasal routes, making it very useful for seizures and time-sensitive sedation when IV access may not exist. Compared with lorazepam or diazepam, its route flexibility is a major prehospital advantage; respiratory depression still requires close monitoring.","Best fit: active seizure or sedation where rapid administration and flexible routes matter.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42640630/"],"10-32":["Why choose morphine?","Morphine is a proven opioid analgesic with a somewhat longer duration than fentanyl, but it releases more histamine and can produce more vasodilation. It may be useful when sustained analgesia is desired in a hemodynamically stable patient; fentanyl is often attractive when faster titration and less histamine effect are priorities.","Best fit: significant pain in a stable patient when a longer opioid effect is useful and protocol allows.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34928760/"],"10-33":["Why choose naloxone?","Naloxone reverses opioid effects, especially respiratory depression. The goal is usually restoration of adequate ventilation\u2014not necessarily making the patient fully awake\u2014because abrupt full reversal can precipitate withdrawal and agitation.","Best fit: suspected opioid toxicity with clinically important respiratory/CNS depression.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441910/"],"10-34":["Why choose nitroglycerin?","Nitroglycerin rapidly reduces preload and, at higher doses, afterload through vasodilation. That makes it particularly useful when pulmonary edema is driven by severe hypertension; in chest pain, blood pressure and contraindications determine whether its vasodilator effect is appropriate.","Best fit: hypertensive acute pulmonary edema or protocol-defined ischemic chest discomfort with adequate pressure.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482382/"],"10-35":["Why choose norepinephrine?","Norepinephrine provides strong alpha vasoconstriction with some beta-1 support and is widely favored as a first-line vasopressor in distributive/septic shock. Compared with epinephrine, it generally produces less beta-2 stimulation and is often easier to titrate primarily for vascular tone.","Best fit: persistent hypotensive shock requiring vasopressor support when the protocol indicates it.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537259/"],"10-36":["Why choose olanzapine?","Olanzapine is an atypical antipsychotic that can calm agitation without relying solely on benzodiazepine sedation. It may be useful when psychosis or behavioral agitation is prominent; benzodiazepines may be preferable for withdrawal or certain stimulant-related states, while ketamine may be used when immediate control of extreme danger is required.","Best fit: protocol-defined agitation where an antipsychotic approach matches the clinical picture.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41355751/"],"10-37":["Why choose oxygen?","Oxygen treats hypoxemia; it is not automatically beneficial simply because a patient is sick. Titrating to the clinical target avoids unnecessary hyperoxia while still correcting inadequate oxygenation.","Best fit: hypoxemia, critical illness, preoxygenation, or another protocol-defined indication.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470453/"],"10-38":["Why choose ondansetron?","Ondansetron is an effective antiemetic with little sedation. In a randomized ED comparison, ondansetron and promethazine relieved nausea similarly, but promethazine caused more sedation\u2014so ondansetron is attractive when you want nausea control without making the patient drowsier.","Best fit: nausea/vomiting when minimizing sedation is useful.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18304050/"],"10-39":["Why choose pralidoxime?","Pralidoxime reactivates acetylcholinesterase when given early in organophosphate poisoning, addressing the underlying enzyme inhibition. Atropine treats the dangerous muscarinic secretions/bronchorrhea, so the two drugs have complementary roles rather than being substitutes.","Best fit: suspected organophosphate/nerve-agent poisoning in conjunction with atropine and protocol care.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558908/"],"10-40":["Why choose procainamide?","Procainamide slows conduction and can terminate certain stable wide-complex tachycardias. Compared with amiodarone, selection depends on the rhythm and patient; procainamide requires close attention to hypotension and QRS widening during infusion.","Best fit: stable monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia when the protocol specifically selects procainamide.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557788/"],"10-41":["Why choose promethazine?","Promethazine treats nausea and also has antihistamine/sedating effects. Compared with ondansetron, nausea relief can be similar, but promethazine causes more sedation\u2014so that sedation may be a drawback when you want the patient alert.","Best fit: nausea when its antihistamine properties are useful and sedation is acceptable under protocol.","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18304050/"],"10-42":["Why choose proparacaine?","Proparacaine is a topical ocular anesthetic that can rapidly relieve corneal surface pain and make eye irrigation/examination easier. Systemic analgesics do not provide the same immediate local corneal anesthesia.","Best fit: painful superficial eye conditions where topical anesthesia is specifically permitted.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470539/"],"10-43":["Why choose rocuronium?","Rocuronium is a nondepolarizing paralytic with a longer duration than succinylcholine and avoids succinylcholine-associated hyperkalemia and malignant-hyperthermia concerns. The tradeoff is prolonged paralysis, so adequate ongoing sedation/analgesia is essential.","Best fit: RSI/paralysis when a longer-acting agent is desirable or succinylcholine is contraindicated.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539888/"],"10-44":["Why choose sodium bicarbonate?","Sodium bicarbonate is not a routine cardiac-arrest drug; it is valuable in specific problems such as sodium-channel blocker toxicity, severe hyperkalemia, or crush-related acidosis/hyperkalemia because its sodium load and alkalinizing effect target those mechanisms.","Best fit: the specific toxicologic/metabolic indications listed in protocol rather than undifferentiated arrest.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559139/"],"10-45":["Why choose succinylcholine?","Succinylcholine has very rapid onset and short paralysis duration, which can be useful for RSI. Rocuronium lasts much longer but avoids important succinylcholine risks such as dangerous hyperkalemia in susceptible patients and malignant hyperthermia.","Best fit: RSI when rapid short-duration paralysis is desired and no contraindication is present.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499984/"],"10-46":["Why choose TXA?","TXA reduces breakdown of formed clots by inhibiting fibrinolysis. It does not replace hemorrhage control or blood; it is an adjunct when significant bleeding is occurring and the protocol timing/eligibility criteria are met.","Best fit: qualifying major hemorrhage where preserving formed clot is beneficial.","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532909/"],"10-47":["Why choose whole blood/blood products?","Blood replaces oxygen-carrying capacity and coagulation components that crystalloid cannot. In hemorrhagic shock, prehospital blood-based resuscitation can better address the actual losses while limiting excessive crystalloid dilution.","Best fit: protocol-defined hemorrhagic shock when blood products are available and indicated.","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12278122/"]};
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s), $$=s=>[...document.querySelectorAll(s)];
const esc=s=>(s??'').toString().replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
const ls={get:(k,d)=>{try{return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(k))??d}catch{return d}},set:(k,v)=>localStorage.setItem(k,JSON.stringify(v))};
const favs=()=>new Set(ls.get('favorites',[]));
function toggleFav(id){let f=favs();f.has(id)?f.delete(id):f.add(id);ls.set('favorites',[...f]);render();}
function stats(){return ls.get('stats',{correct:0,wrong:0,reviewed:0,streak:0,last:'',history:{}})}
function record(id,ok){let st=stats();st.reviewed++;ok?st.correct++:st.wrong++;let d=new Date().toISOString().slice(0,10);if(st.last!==d){let y=new Date(Date.now()-864e5).toISOString().slice(0,10);st.streak=st.last===y?st.streak+1:1;st.last=d}let h=st.history[id]||{ease:2.5,interval:0,due:0};if(ok){h.interval=h.interval===0?1:h.interval===1?3:Math.round(h.interval*h.ease);h.ease=Math.min(3,h.ease+.05)}else{h.interval=0;h.ease=Math.max(1.3,h.ease-.2)}h.due=Date.now()+h.interval*864e5;st.history[id]=h;ls.set('stats',st)}
function buildStepCards(){
  const skip=new Set(['Introduction','Reference Documents','Medication Formulary']);
  const cards=[];
  for(const p of S.protocols){
    if(skip.has(p.category)) continue;
    const lines=(p.body||'').split(/\n+/).map(x=>x.trim()).filter(Boolean);
    const bullets=[];
    for(const raw of lines){
      if(!/^[▪◦▫•]/.test(raw)) continue;
      const text=raw.replace(/^[▪◦▫•]+\s*/,'').replace(/\s+/g,' ').trim();
      if(text.length<18||text.length>170) continue;
      if(/^(adult|pediatric|paramedic|aemt|emt|note|notes|medication|route|dose|max dose)$/i.test(text)) continue;
      bullets.push(text);
    }
    let made=0;
    for(let i=0;i<bullets.length-1 && made<4;i++){
      if(bullets[i]===bullets[i+1]) continue;
      cards.push({id:`seq-${p.id}-${i}`,prompt:`${p.number}: ${p.title}\nWhich listed step follows this one in the education draft?\n“${bullets[i]}”`,answer:bullets[i+1],protocolID:p.id,protocolLabel:`${p.number}: ${p.title}`,sourcePage:p.startPage});
      made++;
    }
  }
  return cards;
}
function dueCount(){let st=stats(),now=Date.now();return [...S.dcards,...S.scenarios,...S.stepcards].filter(c=>(st.history[c.id]?.due||0)<=now).length}

const VISUAL_REFERENCE_PAGES = new Set([36,37,38,62,71,77,82,84,99,113,118,138,152,166,180,215,216,227,229,266,319]);
const MED_VISUALS = {"10-31":[319]};
function visualBlockForPages(pages,label="Reference chart / visual") {
  const valid=[...new Set((pages||[]).filter(p=>VISUAL_REFERENCE_PAGES.has(Number(p))))];
  if(!valid.length)return '';
  return `<div class="block visualRefs"><h3>${esc(label)}</h3><p class="visualHelp">These are source visuals from the uploaded education draft. Tap an image to open it full size.</p>${valid.map(pg=>`<a class="visualLink" href="reference/page-${pg}.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img class="referenceImage" src="reference/page-${pg}.jpg" alt="Source reference visual, page ${pg}" loading="lazy"><span>Open source page ${pg} full size ↗</span></a>`).join('')}</div>`;
}
function protocolVisuals(p){
  const pages=[];
  for(let pg=Number(p.startPage)||0; pg<=(Number(p.endPage)||Number(p.startPage)||0); pg++) if(VISUAL_REFERENCE_PAGES.has(pg)) pages.push(pg);
  return visualBlockForPages(pages,'Reference charts / algorithms');
}
function medicationVisuals(m){ return visualBlockForPages(MED_VISUALS[m.id]||[],'Reference dosing chart'); }

async function load(){Object.assign(S,{protocols:EMBEDDED_DATA.protocols,meds:EMBEDDED_DATA.medications,pcards:EMBEDDED_DATA.protocol_cards,scenarios:EMBEDDED_DATA.scenarios,dcards:EMBEDDED_DATA.dose_cards});S.stepcards=buildStepCards();render()}
function nav(){ $$('#tabs button').forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle('active',b.dataset.tab===S.tab)); }
function setTab(t){S.tab=t;render();scrollTo(0,0)}
function home(){let st=stats(),acc=st.reviewed?Math.round(st.correct/st.reviewed*100):0;return `<section class="hero"><h2>Know the protocol.</h2><p>Learn it, practice it, then apply it to patient scenarios.</p></section><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><b>${dueCount()}</b><span>Due now</span></div><div class="stat"><b>${acc}%</b><span>Accuracy</span></div><div class="stat"><b>${st.streak}</b><span>Day streak</span></div></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Start here</h2><div class="grid homeChoices"><button class="action" onclick="setTab('learn')">◎ Learn<small>Guided levels, due review, doses, scenarios and sequence practice</small></button><button class="action" onclick="openLibrary()">▤ Library<small>Search all ${S.protocols.length} protocols and ${S.meds.length} medications</small></button></div>`}
function learn(){return `<section class="hero"><h2>Learn</h2><p>Study the material first, then use practice questions when you are ready.</p></section><h2 class="sectionTitle">Guided learning</h2><div class="grid learnGrid"><button class="action learnAction" onclick="protocolLessons()">▤ Learn a protocol<small>Work through presentation, priorities, treatment and recall</small></button><button class="action learnAction" onclick="medicationLessons()">✚ Learn a medication<small>Study indications, dosing, cautions and practical context</small></button><button class="action learnAction" onclick="startRecallDeck()">◫ Reveal study cards<small>Try to recall the key material, then reveal the source answer</small></button><button class="action learnAction" onclick="dailyLesson()">◉ Quick lesson<small>One protocol and one medication chosen for today</small></button></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Practice after learning</h2><div class="grid"><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('foundation')">Level 1 · Foundations<small>Medication doses + applied decisions</small></button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('application')">Level 2 · Application<small>Patient scenarios + protocol sequence</small></button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('challenge')">Level 3 · Challenge<small>Doses, decisions & next listed steps</small></button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('due')">Due review<small>Questions scheduled for you to revisit</small></button></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Focused quizzes</h2><div class="grid"><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('dose')">Dose quiz<small>Medication dosing only</small></button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('recognition')">Application quiz<small>Choose the protocol-based action or decision</small></button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('sequence')">Sequence quiz<small>Choose the next listed protocol step</small></button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('mixed')">Mixed 30<small>Random questions from all formats</small></button></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Practice tool</h2><div class="card"><label>Weight conversion</label><input id="lbs" class="search" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="Weight in lb" oninput="calcKg()"><p id="kg">Enter a weight to convert to kg.</p></div>`}

function lessonSearch(title,placeholder,items,rowFn,filterFn){modal(`<div class="detail"><h2>${esc(title)}</h2><p class="lessonIntro">Choose an item and work through it one section at a time. This is guided study, not a graded quiz.</p><input id="lessonSearch" class="search" placeholder="${esc(placeholder)}"><div id="lessonList" class="list">${items.slice(0,60).map(rowFn).join('')}</div></div>`);$('#lessonSearch').oninput=e=>{$('#lessonList').innerHTML=items.filter(x=>filterFn(x,e.target.value.toLowerCase())).slice(0,60).map(rowFn).join('')}}
function protocolLessons(){lessonSearch('Learn a protocol','Search protocols…',S.protocols,p=>`<button class="row" onclick="openProtocolLesson('${p.id}')"><span class="num">${esc(p.number)} · ${esc(p.category)}</span><h3>${esc(p.title)}</h3><p>Guided protocol walkthrough</p></button>`,(p,q)=>`${p.number} ${p.title} ${p.category}`.toLowerCase().includes(q))}
function medicationLessons(){lessonSearch('Learn a medication','Search medications…',S.meds,m=>`<button class="row" onclick="openMedicationLesson('${m.id}')"><span class="num">${esc(m.protocolNumber)}</span><h3>${esc(m.name)}</h3><p>Indications · dosing · cautions</p></button>`,(m,q)=>JSON.stringify(m).toLowerCase().includes(q))}
function lessonSection(n,title,body,prompt){return `<section class="lessonStep"><div class="lessonNumber">${n}</div><div><h3>${esc(title)}</h3>${prompt?`<p class="recallPrompt">Before reading: ${esc(prompt)}</p>`:''}<div class="bodyText">${body}</div></div></section>`}
function openProtocolLesson(id){let p=S.protocols.find(x=>x.id===id);if(!p)return;let overview=p.designation?esc(p.designation):'No separate designation was extracted. Study the opening protocol text below.';modal(`<div class="detail lesson"><span class="num">${esc(p.number)} · ${esc(p.category)}</span><h2>${esc(p.title)}</h2><div class="meta">Guided lesson · education draft source pp. ${p.startPage}${p.endPage!==p.startPage?'–'+p.endPage:''}</div>${lessonSection(1,'Recognize the situation',overview,'What presentation or condition should make you think of this protocol?')}${lessonSection(2,'Find the priorities',`<button class="btn revealBtn" onclick="revealLesson(this)">Reveal protocol priorities and treatment</button><div class="lessonHidden bodyText">${esc(p.body)}</div>`,'What must happen first, and what must not be delayed?')}${protocolVisuals(p)}${lessonSection(3,'Teach it back',`<ul class="teachBack"><li>Describe the patient presentation without naming the protocol.</li><li>State the first priorities in order.</li><li>Name the medication, dose, contraindication, cutoff or destination decision you must remember.</li></ul>`,'Can you explain the protocol aloud without looking?')}<div class="sourceOnly">County draft lesson. No outside pharmacology is used as protocol instruction.</div></div>`)}
function openMedicationLesson(id){let m=S.meds.find(x=>x.id===id);if(!m)return;let indications=(m.indications||[]).map(x=>`<span class="pill">${esc(x)}</span>`).join('')||'<p>See the source administration text.</p>';let cautions=[...(m.contraindications||[]),...(m.specialNotes||[])].map(x=>`<span class="pill">${esc(x)}</span>`).join('')||'<p>No separate caution list was extracted; review the full administration text.</p>';modal(`<div class="detail lesson"><span class="num">${esc(m.protocolNumber)}</span><h2>${esc(m.name)}</h2><div class="meta">Guided lesson · education draft source page ${m.sourcePage}</div>${lessonSection(1,'When it appears in the county draft',indications,'Which patient or protocol indication calls for this medication?')}${lessonSection(2,'Route and dose',`<button class="btn revealBtn" onclick="revealLesson(this)">Reveal routes and dosing</button><div class="lessonHidden"><div>${(m.routes||[]).map(x=>`<span class="pill">${esc(x)}</span>`).join('')}</div><div class="bodyText">${esc(m.administrationText||'')}</div></div>`,'Can you state the adult and pediatric dose, route and maximum?')}${lessonSection(3,'Contraindications and special notes',cautions,'What finding would make you stop, modify the dose or choose another action?')}${medicationVisuals(m)}${practicalNote(m)}<div class="sourceOnly">County dosing and cautions are above. The separately labeled practical note is general pharmacology and is not county protocol instruction.</div></div>`)}
function revealLesson(btn){let x=btn.nextElementSibling;x.classList.remove('lessonHidden');btn.hidden=true}
function startRecallDeck(){let cards=shuffled(S.pcards).slice(0,12);S.recallCards=cards;S.recallIndex=0;showRecallCard()}
function showRecallCard(){let c=S.recallCards?.[S.recallIndex];if(!c){closeModal();return}modal(`<div class="detail lesson"><div class="metric"><b>Reveal study cards</b><span>${S.recallIndex+1}/${S.recallCards.length}</span></div><div class="progress"><div style="width:${(S.recallIndex+1)/S.recallCards.length*100}%"></div></div><section class="recallCard"><p class="recallPrompt">Try to answer from memory before revealing it.</p><h2>${esc(c.prompt)}</h2><button class="btn primary" onclick="revealRecall(this)">Reveal source answer</button><div id="recallAnswer" class="lessonHidden answer"><div class="bodyText">${esc(c.answer)}</div><small>Education draft source page ${c.sourcePage}</small><div class="buttons recallButtons"><button class="btn bad" onclick="nextRecall()">Review again</button><button class="btn good" onclick="nextRecall()">I remembered it</button></div></div></section></div>`)}
function revealRecall(btn){$('#recallAnswer').classList.remove('lessonHidden');btn.hidden=true}
function nextRecall(){S.recallIndex++;showRecallCard()}
function dailyLesson(){let day=Math.floor(Date.now()/86400000),p=S.protocols[day%S.protocols.length],m=S.meds[day%S.meds.length];modal(`<div class="detail lesson"><h2>Quick lesson</h2><p class="lessonIntro">One protocol and one medication for today. Read each, then explain the essentials aloud.</p><button class="row" onclick="openProtocolLesson('${p.id}')"><span class="num">Today's protocol · ${esc(p.number)}</span><h3>${esc(p.title)}</h3><p>Presentation → priorities → treatment → teach-back</p></button><button class="row" onclick="openMedicationLesson('${m.id}')"><span class="num">Today's medication</span><h3>${esc(m.name)}</h3><p>Indications → dosing → cautions → practical context</p></button></div>`)}
function openLibrary(kind=''){S.libraryView=kind;S.tab='library';render();scrollTo(0,0)}
function library(){if(S.libraryView==='protocols')return `<button class="btn" onclick="openLibrary()">‹ Library</button><h2 class="sectionTitle">Protocols</h2>${protocolList()}`;if(S.libraryView==='meds')return `<button class="btn" onclick="openLibrary()">‹ Library</button><h2 class="sectionTitle">Medications</h2>${medsList()}`;let f=favs();return `<section class="hero"><h2>Library</h2><p>Search the county draft and medication pages from one place.</p></section><h2 class="sectionTitle">Browse</h2><div class="grid"><button class="action" onclick="openLibrary('protocols')">${S.protocols.length} protocols<small>Search sections, procedures and source text</small></button><button class="action" onclick="openLibrary('meds')">${S.meds.length} medications<small>Doses, indications, cautions and reference charts</small></button></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Saved</h2><div class="card"><p>${[...f].length} favorites saved on this device.</p><button class="btn primary" onclick="showFavorites()">View favorites</button></div>`}
function protocolList(){let cats=['All',...new Set(S.protocols.map(x=>x.category))];return `<input id="q" class="search" placeholder="Search protocols…" oninput="filterProtocols()"><div class="chips" id="pcats">${cats.map((c,i)=>`<button class="chip ${i?'':'active'}" onclick="pickPCat(this,'${esc(c).replace(/'/g,"\\'")}')">${esc(c)}</button>`).join('')}</div><div id="plist" class="list">${protoRows(S.protocols)}</div>`}
function protoRows(a){return a.map(p=>`<button class="row" onclick="openProtocol('${p.id}')"><span class="num">${esc(p.number)} · ${esc(p.category)}</span><h3>${esc(p.title)}</h3><p>Source pp. ${p.startPage}${p.endPage&&p.endPage!==p.startPage?'–'+p.endPage:''}</p></button>`).join('')}
let pcat='All';function pickPCat(el,c){pcat=c;$$('#pcats .chip').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('active'));el.classList.add('active');filterProtocols()}
function filterProtocols(){let q=($('#q')?.value||'').toLowerCase();let a=S.protocols.filter(p=>(pcat==='All'||p.category===pcat)&&(`${p.number} ${p.title} ${p.body}`.toLowerCase().includes(q)));$('#plist').innerHTML=protoRows(a)}
function medsList(){return `<input id="mq" class="search" placeholder="Search medications…" oninput="filterMeds()"><div id="mlist" class="list">${medRows(S.meds)}</div>`}
function medRows(a){let f=favs();return a.map(m=>{let routes=(m.routes||[]).filter(x=>x && !x.includes(':') && x.length<40).slice(0,5);return `<button class="row" onclick="openMed('${m.id}')"><span class="num">${esc(m.protocolNumber)} ${f.has('m:'+m.id)?'★':''}</span><h3>${esc(m.name)}</h3><p class="routeLine">${esc(routes.join(' · '))}</p></button>`}).join('')}
function filterMeds(){let q=($('#mq')?.value||'').toLowerCase();$('#mlist').innerHTML=medRows(S.meds.filter(m=>JSON.stringify(m).toLowerCase().includes(q)))}
function listBlock(title,arr){return arr?.length?`<div class="block"><h3>${title}</h3>${arr.map(x=>`<span class="pill">${esc(x)}</span>`).join('')}</div>`:''}
function practicalNote(m){let n=PRACTICAL_NOTES[m.id];if(!n)return '';let [title,why,best,url]=n;return `<div class="block practical"><h3>🩺 ${esc(title)}</h3><div class="bodyText">${esc(why)}</div><div class="bestfit"><strong>${esc(best.split(':')[0])}:</strong>${esc(best.slice(best.indexOf(':')+1))}</div><div class="evidenceTag">General pharmacology / evidence note — not Snohomish County protocol instruction.</div><a class="sourceLink" href="${esc(url)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View supporting medical source ↗</a></div>`}
function openMed(id){let m=S.meds.find(x=>x.id===id),f=favs().has('m:'+id);modal(`<div class="detail"><button class="fav" onclick="toggleFav('m:${id}');openMed('${id}')">${f?'★':'☆'}</button><span class="num">${esc(m.protocolNumber)}</span><h2>${esc(m.name)}</h2><div class="meta">Education draft · source page ${m.sourcePage}</div>${listBlock('Class',m.drugClass)}${listBlock('Routes',m.routes)}${listBlock('Indications',m.indications)}${listBlock('Contraindications',m.contraindications)}<div class="block"><h3>Administration / dosing</h3><div class="bodyText">${esc(m.administrationText)}</div></div>${listBlock('Special notes',m.specialNotes)}${medicationVisuals(m)}${practicalNote(m)}</div>`)}
function openProtocol(id){let p=S.protocols.find(x=>x.id===id),f=favs().has('p:'+id);modal(`<div class="detail"><button class="fav" onclick="toggleFav('p:${id}');openProtocol('${id}')">${f?'★':'☆'}</button><span class="num">${esc(p.number)} · ${esc(p.category)}</span><h2>${esc(p.title)}</h2><div class="meta">Education draft · source pp. ${p.startPage}${p.endPage!==p.startPage?'–'+p.endPage:''}</div>${p.designation?`<div class="block"><h3>Designation / overview</h3><div class="bodyText">${esc(p.designation)}</div></div>`:''}<div class="block"><h3>Protocol text</h3><div class="bodyText">${esc(p.body)}</div></div>${protocolVisuals(p)}</div>`)}
function modal(html){$('#modalBody').innerHTML=html;$('#modal').classList.remove('hidden')}
function closeModal(){$('#modal').classList.add('hidden')}
function tagged(arr,type){return arr.map(c=>({...c,type}))}
function shuffled(a){a=[...a];for(let i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){let j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]}return a}
function makeQueue(mode){
  const doses=tagged(S.dcards,'Dose'), recognition=tagged(S.scenarios,'Application'), sequence=tagged(S.stepcards,'Sequence');
  let arr=[];
  if(mode==='dose')arr=doses;
  else if(mode==='recognition')arr=recognition;
  else if(mode==='sequence')arr=sequence;
  else if(mode==='foundation')arr=[...shuffled(doses).slice(0,10),...shuffled(recognition).slice(0,5)];
  else if(mode==='application')arr=[...shuffled(recognition).slice(0,8),...shuffled(sequence).slice(0,8),...shuffled(doses).slice(0,4)];
  else if(mode==='challenge')arr=[...shuffled(sequence).slice(0,12),...shuffled(recognition).slice(0,9),...shuffled(doses).slice(0,9)];
  else if(mode==='due'){let now=Date.now(),st=stats();arr=[...doses,...recognition,...sequence].filter(c=>(st.history[c.id]?.due||0)<=now)}
  else arr=[...doses,...recognition,...sequence];
  return shuffled(arr).slice(0,30)
}
function startStudy(mode){S.mode=mode;S.queue=makeQueue(mode);S.idx=0;resetQuestion();S.tab='learn';render()}
function resetQuestion(){S.show=false;S.choices=[];S.selected=null;S.answered=false}
function choicePool(c){let src=c.type==='Dose'?S.dcards:c.type==='Sequence'?S.stepcards:S.scenarios;let vals=[...new Set(src.map(x=>x.answer).filter(Boolean))].filter(x=>x!==c.answer);return shuffled(vals)}
function ensureChoices(c){if(S.choices.length)return;S.choices=c.choices?shuffled(c.choices):shuffled([c.answer,...choicePool(c).slice(0,3)])}
function answerChoice(i){if(S.answered)return;let c=S.queue[S.idx];S.selected=i;S.answered=true;record(c.id,S.choices[i]===c.answer);render()}
function nextQuestion(){S.idx++;resetQuestion();render()}
function modeTitle(){return ({foundation:'Level 1 · Foundations',application:'Level 2 · Application',challenge:'Level 3 · Challenge',dose:'Dose quiz',recognition:'Application quiz',sequence:'Sequence quiz',due:'Due review',mixed:'Mixed quiz'})[S.mode]||'Quiz'}
function study(){if(!S.queue.length)return `<h2>Quiz</h2><div class="card"><p>Choose a multiple-choice training mode.</p><div class="grid"><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('foundation')">Level 1 · Foundations</button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('application')">Level 2 · Application</button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('challenge')">Level 3 · Challenge</button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('due')">Due review</button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('dose')">Dose quiz</button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('recognition')">Application quiz</button><button class="action" onclick="startStudy('sequence')">Sequence quiz</button></div></div>`;let c=S.queue[S.idx];if(!c)return `<div class="card"><h2>Quiz complete</h2><p>Your results were saved on this device.</p><button class="btn primary" onclick="startStudy('${S.mode}')">Quiz again</button></div>`;ensureChoices(c);let ok=S.answered&&S.choices[S.selected]===c.answer;return `<div class="metric"><b>${esc(modeTitle())}</b><span>${S.idx+1}/${S.queue.length}</span></div><div class="progress"><div style="width:${(S.idx+1)/S.queue.length*100}%"></div></div><div style="height:12px"></div><section class="studyCard"><div><div class="label">${esc(c.questionKind||c.type)} · choose the best answer</div><h2>${esc(c.prompt)}</h2><div class="mcq">${S.choices.map((x,i)=>{let cls='choice';if(S.answered){if(x===c.answer)cls+=' correct';else if(i===S.selected)cls+=' wrong'}return `<button class="${cls}" onclick="answerChoice(${i})" ${S.answered?'disabled':''}><span>${String.fromCharCode(65+i)}</span><div>${esc(x)}</div></button>`}).join('')}</div>${S.answered?`<div class="feedback ${ok?'goodFeedback':'badFeedback'}"><b>${ok?'Correct':'Not quite'}</b><p>Answer: ${esc(c.answer)}</p>${c.explanation?`<p>${esc(c.explanation)}</p>`:''}${c.protocolLabel?`<small>${esc(c.protocolLabel)} · education draft page ${c.sourcePage}</small>`:c.sourcePage?`<small>Education draft source page ${c.sourcePage}</small>`:''}</div>`:''}</div>${S.answered?`<button class="btn primary" onclick="nextQuestion()">${S.idx+1===S.queue.length?'Finish quiz':'Next question'}</button>`:''}</section>`}
function more(){let st=stats(),total=st.reviewed,acc=total?Math.round(st.correct/total*100):0,f=favs();return `<h2>Progress</h2><div class="card"><div class="metric"><span>Cards reviewed</span><b>${total}</b></div><div class="metric"><span>Correct</span><b>${st.correct}</b></div><div class="metric"><span>Accuracy</span><b>${acc}%</b></div><div class="metric"><span>Current streak</span><b>${st.streak} days</b></div></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Favorites</h2><div class="card"><p>${[...f].length} saved items</p><button class="btn primary" onclick="showFavorites()">View favorites</button></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">Dose calculator practice</h2><div class="card"><label>Weight (lb)</label><input id="lbs" class="search" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="e.g. 44" oninput="calcKg()"><p id="kg">Enter a weight to convert to kg.</p></div><h2 class="sectionTitle">About</h2><div class="card bodyText">This app is a study aid built from the uploaded Snohomish County EMS V7 education draft. It is not an approved clinical reference and should not be used for patient care. Study progress and favorites are stored only on this device.</div>`}
function calcKg(){let x=parseFloat($('#lbs').value);$('#kg').textContent=isFinite(x)?`${(x/2.2046226218).toFixed(1)} kg`:'Enter a weight to convert to kg.'}
function showFavorites(){let f=favs(),p=S.protocols.filter(x=>f.has('p:'+x.id)),m=S.meds.filter(x=>f.has('m:'+x.id));modal(`<div class="detail"><h2>Favorites</h2><h3 class="sectionTitle">Protocols</h3><div class="list">${protoRows(p)||'<p>None yet.</p>'}</div><h3 class="sectionTitle">Medications</h3><div class="list">${medRows(m)||'<p>None yet.</p>'}</div></div>`)}
function render(){nav();let v=S.tab==='home'?home():S.tab==='learn'?(S.queue.length?study():learn()):library();$('#main').innerHTML=v;nav()}
$$('#tabs button').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>setTab(b.dataset.tab));$('#closeModal').onclick=closeModal;$('#modal').onclick=e=>{if(e.target.id==='modal')closeModal()};
window.setTab=setTab;window.openLibrary=openLibrary;window.protocolLessons=protocolLessons;window.medicationLessons=medicationLessons;window.openProtocolLesson=openProtocolLesson;window.openMedicationLesson=openMedicationLesson;window.revealLesson=revealLesson;window.startRecallDeck=startRecallDeck;window.revealRecall=revealRecall;window.nextRecall=nextRecall;window.dailyLesson=dailyLesson;window.startStudy=startStudy;window.filterProtocols=filterProtocols;window.pickPCat=pickPCat;window.openProtocol=openProtocol;window.openMed=openMed;window.toggleFav=toggleFav;window.filterMeds=filterMeds;window.answerChoice=answerChoice;window.nextQuestion=nextQuestion;window.showFavorites=showFavorites;window.calcKg=calcKg;window.S=S;
let deferredPrompt;window.addEventListener('beforeinstallprompt',e=>{e.preventDefault();deferredPrompt=e;$('#installBtn').hidden=false});$('#installBtn').onclick=async()=>{if(deferredPrompt){deferredPrompt.prompt();deferredPrompt=null}else alert('On iPhone: tap Share in Safari, then Add to Home Screen.')};
if('serviceWorker' in navigator)navigator.serviceWorker.register('sw.js');
load().catch(e=>{$('#main').innerHTML=`<div class="card"><h2>Could not load app data</h2><p>${esc(e.message)}</p><p>Please refresh the page. If this persists, replace the deployed files with the latest app build.</p></div>`});
